  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0180 database is designed to track and manage information about aircraft and their traffic patterns. The aircraft table stores detailed information about each aircraft, including its unique identifier, registration number, manufacturer, model, and operational details such as the owner, operator, and status. The table also captures the aircraft's technical specifications, including its serial number, engine type, and whether it is equipped with ADS-B, ACARS, and other advanced systems. The registration and deregistration dates, as well as the aircraft's first flight date and built date, are also recorded. Additionally, the table includes a category description and notes field for any miscellaneous information. The traffic table, on the other hand, tracks the movement of aircraft, storing information such as the callsign, flight number, and type code, as well as the aircraft's current and previous locations, including latitude and longitude coordinates, and altitude. The table also captures the origin and destination of each flight, along with the date and time of the first and last sightings.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nThe project involves creating and querying a comprehensive database on Google Cloud Platform (GCP) to analyze the impact of COVID-19 on air traffic. This analysis focuses on flying hours over the period from December 2019 to November 2020, particularly from the perspective of engine manufacturers. The dataset includes detailed information on flights and aircraft.\n\n2) Objectives\n\nThe primary objective is to determine the effect of COVID-19 on global air traffic, specifically analyzing usage patterns and downtime from an engine manufacturer's point of view. This involves extracting insights from the data to understand changes in flight operations and aircraft utilization during the specified timeframe.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Engine Manufacturers: Interested in understanding the impact on engine usage and maintenance cycles.\n- Aviation Analysts: Require data to analyze trends in air traffic.\n- Data Scientists and Researchers: Use the database for further studies on the aviation industry's dynamics during COVID-19.\n- Business Decision Makers: Leverage insights for strategic planning and forecasting.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Establish a GCP-based database system to store and manage flight and aircraft data.\n- Implement functionalities to query for flying hours, operations, and grounded statuses of aircraft.\n- Enable data retrieval to generate reports on flight patterns and their correlation with engine usage.\n- Provide tools for analyzing temporal changes in air traffic before, during, and after the peak of the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- The system should ensure efficient query execution to handle large datasets.\n- Maintain data integrity and reliability, with robust backup and recovery mechanisms.\n- Ensure compliance with data privacy standards and regulations.\n- Optimize performance for high availability and scalability to accommodate potential increases in data volume and user access.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0180.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0180\nCREATE TABLE `aircraft` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `registration_number` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `manufacturer` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `model` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `owner` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `operator` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `serial_number` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `engine_type` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `ads_b_equipped` tinyint(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `acars_equipped` tinyint(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `registration_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `deregistration_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `first_flight_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `built_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `category_description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `notes` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `traffic` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `callsign` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `flight_number` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `type_code` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `current_latitude` decimal(9,6) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `current_longitude` decimal(9,6) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `previous_latitude` decimal(9,6) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `previous_longitude` decimal(9,6) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `altitude` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `origin` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `destination` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `first_sighting` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_sighting` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `traffic_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`flight_number`) REFERENCES `aircraft` (`registration_number`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
